17-OBJECTIVE: Familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) is associated with a high risk of premature atherosclerotic cardiovascular 
18-disease (ASCVD). However, this risk is highly heterogeneous and current risk prediction algorithms for FH suffer from 
19-limitations. The primary objective of this study was to develop a score predicting incident ASCVD events over 10 years in 
20-a large multinational FH cohort. The secondary objective was to investigate the prediction of major adverse cardiovascular 
21-events and cardiovascular mortality using this score.
22-APPROACH AND RESULTS:  We prospectively followed 3881 patients with adult heterozygous FH with no prior history of ASCVD 
23-(32 361 person-years of follow-up) from 5 registries in Europe and North America. The FH-Risk-Score incorporates 7 
24:clinical variables: sex, age, high-density lipoprotein cholesterol, low-density lipoprotein cholesterol, hypertension, smoking, 
25:and lipoprotein (a) (Lp(a)) with a Harrell C-index for 10-year ASCVD event of 0.75, which was superior to the SAFEHEART-
26-RE (Spanish Familial Hypercholesterolemia Cohort; 0.69). Subjects with an elevated FH-Risk-Score had decreases in 10-
27:year ASCVD-free survival, 10-year major adverse cardiovascular event-free survival, and 30-year survival for CV mortality 
28:compared with the low-risk group, with hazard ratios of 5.52 (3.94–7 .73), 4.64 (2.66–8.11), and 10.73 (2.51–45.79), 
29-respectively. The FH-Risk-Score showed a similar performance in subjects with and without an FH-causing mutation.
30-CONCLUSIONS:  The FH-Risk-Score is a stronger predictor of future ASCVD than the SAFEHEART-RE and was developed in 
31:FH subjects with no prior cardiovascular event. Furthermore, the FH-Risk-Score is the first score to predict CV death and 
32-could offer personalized cardiovascular risk assessment and treatment for patients with FH. Future studies are required to 
33-validate the FH-Risk-Score in different ethnic groups.
34-GRAPHIC ABSTRACT: A graphic abstract is available for this article.
35:Key Words: adult ◼ cardiovascular disease ◼ hyperlipoproteinemia type II ◼ risk factor ◼ risk prediction
