37-amilial hypercholesterolemia (FH) is a frequent 
38-(1:311) autosomal codominant genetic disorder most 
39-often caused by mutations in either the LDLR, APOB, 
40-or PCSK9 genes.1–3 This disease is associated with a life-
41:long elevation of low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-
42-C) concentration (>95th percentile for age and sex) that 
43-increases the risk of premature and recurrent atheroscle-
44-rotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) when left untreated.4 
45-It has been shown that carriers of an FH-causing mutation 
46-have a greater ASCVD risk compared with noncarriers for 
47-similar levels of LDL-C.5,6 The risk of coronary heart dis-
