61-condition.11 Indeed, several clinical and genetic risk factors 
62-have been shown to modulate ASCVD risk in FH.12 Bet-
63-ter cardiovascular risk estimation is therefore needed for 
64-FH subjects to individualize therapies to the patient’s risk.13 
65-Unfortunately, existing ASCVD risk calculators such as 
66:the Framingham Risk Score, the Pooled Cohort Equation, 
67-and the European Systematic COronary Risk Evaluation 
68-(SCORE) were not developed in FH cohorts and under-
69-estimate the ASCVD risk in these patients. 14 The Mon-
70-treal-FH-SCORE is an ASCVD risk calculator designed 
71-specifically for the FH population that uses 5 simple clini-
72:cal variables, namely age, high-density lipoprotein choles-
73-terol (HDL-C), sex, arterial hypertension, and smoking to 
74-stratify the risk of ASCVD.15,16 However, this score has only 
75-been developed and validated in retrospective cohorts. The 
76-SAFEHEART-RE (Spanish Familial Hypercholesterolemia 
77-Cohort) was developed in a prospective cohort of Spanish 
78-FH subjects, but one which included both subjects in pri-
79-mary and secondary cardiovascular prevention.17 ,18
80-The primary objective of the present study was to 
81-develop a new score to predict incident cardiovascu-
82-lar events in a large multinational prospective cohort of 
83-patients with FH without prior history of cardiovascular 
84-events. Furthermore, we aimed at studying the prediction 
85:of major adverse cardiovascular events (MACEs) and 
86-cardiovascular mortality using this score.
