301-P , .0001).
302-Discussion
303-Previous studies have shown that classical risk factors
304-such as age, male gender, smoking, hypertension, diabetes,
305:and lipoprotein parameters are all predictors of CVD risk in
306-FH populations, 3,20 but no study has looked at the com-
307-bined effect of these risk factors. We propose a novel clin-
308-ical score to help predict CVD events in FH patients by
309-combining classical risk factors. This approach is some-
310-what similar to the point scoring system used to predict car-
311-diovascular risk in non-FH patients such as the
312:Framingham Risk Score, the European SCORE, or the Rey-
313:nolds Risk Score.
314-The Montreal-FH-SCORE exhibits a good predictive
315-accuracy with an AUC of 0.840, which is similar to other
316-diagnostic scores used in clinic. For comparison, the
317:Framingham Risk Score had an AUC of 0.776 and 0.834
318-(for men and women, respectively)
319-29 for the prediction of
320-CVD events in the general population. Most of the predic-
321-tive value of our model originates from age, and this is not
322-unexpected because the underlying physiopathological
323-mechanism of CVD in FH is cholesterol-year exposure.
