23-BACKGROUND: Familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) is a disease characterized by increased low-
24:density lipoprotein cholesterol and premature cardiovascular disease (CVD) but there is marked indi-
25-viduality in the occurrence of CVD events. Recently, the Montreal-FH-SCORE (MFHS) has been
26-shown to stratify CVD frequency in FH subjects, but this score has not yet been validated.
27-OBJECTIVE: The aims of the present study were to conduct an independent external validation of
28-the MFHS in a retrospective cohort of heterozygous FH and to identify additional variables that could
29-signiﬁcantly improve the prediction of prevalent CVD.
30:METHODS: The MFHS calculation is based on 5 variables: age, high-density lipoprotein choles-
31-terol, gender, hypertension, and smoking status. This score was validated in a cohort of 718 adult
32-FH using receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves analysis to determine the discriminatory abil-
33-ity of the MFHS. The performance of the MFHS was compared to a novel Combined-FH-SCORE in
34-1388 FH.
35-RESULTS: The MFHS had an excellent discrimination for prevalent CVD events in the validation
36-cohort, with an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve of 0.799 (0.766–0.832,
37-P , .0001). Patients with a high MFHS score presented a signiﬁcant 8.8-fold increased odd of
38-CVD events compared with patients with a low score (95% conﬁdence interval 5.8–13.3,
39:P , .0001). The addition of lipoprotein(a) to the score did not improve the prediction of CVD events
40-(area under the receiver operating characteristic curve 0.823 vs 0.817, P 5 .11).
41-CONCLUSION: This study conﬁrmed that the MFHS is a strong predictor of prevalent CVD in FH
42:and that the addition of lipoprotein(a) offers a minor improvement in the discrimination of the score.
