58-Introduction
59-Familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) is the most preva-
60-lent autosomal co-dominant genetic disease worldwide with
61-an estimated frequency of 1:250 to 1:500.
62-1–3 This disease is
63:mainly caused by mutations in low-density lipoprotein
64:(LDL) receptor ( LDLR), apolipoprotein B ( ApoB), or pro-
65-protein convertase subtilisin/kexin-type 9 genes. Typically,
66-FH leads to extremely high plasma LDL cholesterol (LDL-
67-C) concentrations and high lifetime risk of cardiovascular
68-disease (CVD). Indeed, coronary symptoms will be present
69-in 45% of men and 20% of women with heterozygous FH
70-(referred as FH in this article) before the age of 50 years.
--
75-FH is variable and circulating LDL-C level is not the sole
76-indicator for risk prediction.
77-3,6–11 CVD risk assessment
78-and intensive risk factors management in FH should there-
79-fore be the cornerstone of cardiovascular prevention. The
80-current established cardiovascular risk estimators such as
81:the Framingham Risk Score, the Pooled Cohort Equation,
82-and the European SCORE were created to assess CVD
83-risk in the general population and cannot be applied to
84-FH subjects.
85-12 The recently developed Montreal-FH-
86-SCORE (MFHS) 13 represents the ﬁrst scoring tool used
87-to stratify CVD prevalence in FH subjects. This point
88-scoring system uses 5 variables, namely age, high-density
89:lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C), gender, hypertension,
90-and smoking. The MFHS showed an excellent association
91-with prevalent CVD with an area under the receiver oper-
92-ating characteristic (ROC) curve (AUC) of 0.84 in the
93-development cohort of 670 French Canadian adult FH.
94-However, the MFHS has never been validated in an external
95-cohort of FH patients.
