CLINICAL AND POPULATION STUDIES
Familial Hypercholesterolemia-Risk-Score: A 
New Score Predicting Cardiovascular Events 
and Cardiovascular Mortality in Familial 
Hypercholesterolemia
Martine Paquette , Sophie Bernard, Bertrand Cariou , Robert A. Hegele , Jacques Genest, Mark Trinder,  
Liam R. Brunham , Sophie Béliard, Alexis Baass
OBJECTIVE: Familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) is associated with a high risk of premature atherosclerotic cardiovascular 
disease (ASCVD). However, this risk is highly heterogeneous and current risk prediction algorithms for FH suffer from 
limitations. The primary objective of this study was to develop a score predicting incident ASCVD events over 10 years in 
a large multinational FH cohort. The secondary objective was to investigate the prediction of major adverse cardiovascular 
events and cardiovascular mortality using this score.
APPROACH AND RESULTS:  We prospectively followed 3881 patients with adult heterozygous FH with no prior history of ASCVD 
(32 361 person-years of follow-up) from 5 registries in Europe and North America. The FH-Risk-Score incorporates 7 
clinical variables: sex, age, high-density lipoprotein cholesterol, low-density lipoprotein cholesterol, hypertension, smoking, 
and lipoprotein (a) (Lp(a)) with a Harrell C-index for 10-year ASCVD event of 0.75, which was superior to the SAFEHEART-
RE (Spanish Familial Hypercholesterolemia Cohort; 0.69). Subjects with an elevated FH-Risk-Score had decreases in 10-
year ASCVD-free survival, 10-year major adverse cardiovascular event-free survival, and 30-year survival for CV mortality 
compared with the low-risk group, with hazard ratios of 5.52 (3.94–7 .73), 4.64 (2.66–8.11), and 10.73 (2.51–45.79), 
respectively. The FH-Risk-Score showed a similar performance in subjects with and without an FH-causing mutation.
CONCLUSIONS:  The FH-Risk-Score is a stronger predictor of future ASCVD than the SAFEHEART-RE and was developed in 
FH subjects with no prior cardiovascular event. Furthermore, the FH-Risk-Score is the first score to predict CV death and 
could offer personalized cardiovascular risk assessment and treatment for patients with FH. Future studies are required to 
validate the FH-Risk-Score in different ethnic groups.
GRAPHIC ABSTRACT: A graphic abstract is available for this article.
Key Words: adult ◼ cardiovascular disease ◼ hyperlipoproteinemia type II ◼ risk factor ◼ risk prediction
F
amilial hypercholesterolemia (FH) is a frequent 
(1:311) autosomal codominant genetic disorder most 
often caused by mutations in either the LDLR, APOB, 
or PCSK9 genes.1–3 This disease is associated with a life-
long elevation of low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-
C) concentration (>95th percentile for age and sex) that 
increases the risk of premature and recurrent atheroscle-
rotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) when left untreated.4 
It has been shown that carriers of an FH-causing mutation 
have a greater ASCVD risk compared with noncarriers for 
similar levels of LDL-C.5,6 The risk of coronary heart dis-
ease can be decreased significantly with statin therapy,7 
particularly with earlier initiation of therapy.8,9 Despite these 
findings, other studies have shown that statin treated FH 
subjects still have significant residual cardiovascular risk.10
Despite a high cumulative LDL cholesterol burden, not 
all patients with FH will develop ASCVD to the same extent, 
emphasizing the wide phenotypic heterogeneity of this 
