 CLINICAL AND POPULATION 
STUDIES - AL
Paquette et al The FH-Risk-Score to Predict ASCVD Risk in FH
Arterioscler Thromb Vasc Biol. 2021;41:2632–2640. DOI: 10.1161/ATVBAHA.121.316106 October 2021  2633
condition.11 Indeed, several clinical and genetic risk factors 
have been shown to modulate ASCVD risk in FH.12 Bet-
ter cardiovascular risk estimation is therefore needed for 
FH subjects to individualize therapies to the patient’s risk.13 
Unfortunately, existing ASCVD risk calculators such as 
the Framingham Risk Score, the Pooled Cohort Equation, 
and the European Systematic COronary Risk Evaluation 
(SCORE) were not developed in FH cohorts and under-
estimate the ASCVD risk in these patients. 14 The Mon-
treal-FH-SCORE is an ASCVD risk calculator designed 
specifically for the FH population that uses 5 simple clini-
cal variables, namely age, high-density lipoprotein choles-
terol (HDL-C), sex, arterial hypertension, and smoking to 
stratify the risk of ASCVD.15,16 However, this score has only 
been developed and validated in retrospective cohorts. The 
SAFEHEART-RE (Spanish Familial Hypercholesterolemia 
Cohort) was developed in a prospective cohort of Spanish 
FH subjects, but one which included both subjects in pri-
mary and secondary cardiovascular prevention.17 ,18
The primary objective of the present study was to 
develop a new score to predict incident cardiovascu-
lar events in a large multinational prospective cohort of 
patients with FH without prior history of cardiovascular 
events. Furthermore, we aimed at studying the prediction 
of major adverse cardiovascular events (MACEs) and 
cardiovascular mortality using this score.
MATERIALS AND METHODS
The data that support the findings of this study are available 
from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.
Study Population and Outcomes
This is a multicenter and multinational prospective cohort study. 
Subjects included in the present study were drawn from 5 pro-
spective registries of patients with FH: the Montreal Clinical 
Research Institute FH cohort (n=482), the FH registry from 
British Columbia (n=335), the UK Biobank (n=499), the Robarts 
Research Institute in Ontario (n=100), and the REFERCHOL 
FH registry from France (n=2465). Detailed description of 
these cohorts is available in Methods19 in the Data Supplement. 
All subjects had at least a probable diagnosis of FH accord-
ing to the Dutch Lipid Clinic Network criteria (≥6 points), while 
the majority (74%) had definite FH with a positive molecular 
diagnosis. Subjects included in this study had no prior history 
of ASCVD. Subjects with a diagnosis of homozygous FH, those 
treated with LDL apheresis, and subjects under 18 years or 
over 65 years of age at baseline were excluded. Cardiovascular 
end points and data concerning the ASCVD predictors were 
collected by the treating physician during the patient’s clinical 
visits independently of the analysis of the data. Cardiovascular 
end points were individually validated using data from the clini-
cal chart, including data from emergency room visits, cardiology 
consultations, and testing (stress test, sestamibi scan, coronary 
angiography etc). Active smoking status was defined as current 
smoking, whereas ever smoking status was established for past, 
occasional, or current smokers. Statin intensity was considered 
low if the expected percent reduction in LDL-C according to 
dose was <30%, moderate if 30% to 50%, and high if >50%.20 
Lipid-lowering therapy (LL T) taken into account included ezeti-
mibe, statins, and PCSK9 inhibitors. Each patient signed an 
informed consent approved by the institutional review board and 
ethics committees from each study site. The study conforms to 
the ethical guidelines of the 1975 Declaration of Helsinki.
End Point Definition
The primary end point was the incidence of any ASCVD event 
at 10 years of follow-up. ASCVD included stable and unstable 
angina, myocardial infarction (MI), coronary angioplasty, coronary 
artery bypass graft, claudication, peripheral angioplasty, peripheral 
arterial surgery, transient ischemic attack, stroke, carotid endarter-
ectomy, carotid angioplasty, and cardiovascular death. Coronary 
artery disease included angina, unstable angina, MI, coronary 
angioplasty, coronary artery bypass graft, and coronary death. The 
3-point MACEs (MACE-3) was defined as a composite of MI, 
stroke, or cardiovascular death, whereas 5-point MACEs (MACE-
5) was defined as MI, stroke, coronary revascularization, hospital 
admission for unstable angina, or cardiovascular death. Coronary 
revascularization was defined as coronary angioplasty or coronary 
bypass surgery. For each individual outcome, only those with suf-
ficient power were presented (at least 10 events). Mortality end 
points were studied after 30 years follow-up because of the low 
number of these events in the cohort at 10 years.
Statistical Analyses
The SPSS statistical software versions 25 and 26 (IBM Corp, 
Armonk, NY), as well as R-software 4.0.0 were used for statistical 
Nonstandard Abbreviations and Acronyms
ASCVD atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease
FH familial hypercholesterolemia
HDL-C high-density lipoprotein cholesterol
LDL-C low-density lipoprotein cholesterol
LLT lipid-lowering therapy
Lp(a) lipoprotein (a)
MACE major adverse cardiovascular event
MI myocardial infarction
Highlights
• Familial hypercholesterolemia (FH) is associated 
with a lifelong elevation in low-density lipoprotein 
cholesterol.
• The atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease risk in 
FH is high but heterogeneous between individuals.
• Sex, age, high-density lipoprotein cholesterol, low-
density lipoprotein cholesterol, hypertension, smok-
ing, and lipoprotein (a) are independent predictors of 
10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease in FH.
• The FH-Risk-Score is a strong predictor of future 
atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease events and 
cardiovascular mortality in FH.
