Paquette et al The FH-Risk-Score to Predict ASCVD Risk in FH
2634  October 2021 Arterioscler Thromb Vasc Biol. 2021;41:2632–2640. DOI: 10.1161/ATVBAHA.121.316106
analysis. Statistical significance was set to P<0·05. Analysis have 
been performed according to TRIPOD statement for Transparent 
Reporting of a Multivariable Prediction Model for Individual 
Prognosis or Diagnosis.30,31 The TRIPOD checklist is presented 
in Table I in the Data Supplement. Further details of the statistical 
method can be obtained in Methods in the Data Supplement.
Model Development and Validation
To predict the 10-year ASCVD risk, a regression equation was 
generated using the log HR (beta coefficient from the Cox 
regression) for each risk factor. Risk categories were generated 
for continuous variables (age, HDL-C and LDL-C). The pres-
ence of each predictor, weighted by its beta coefficient, was 
combined in the formula. The overall 10-year ASCVD probability 
included in the equation was generated using the Kaplan-Meier 
estimate. In addition to the regression formula, we derived an 
associated simple point chart using the same beta-coefficients. 
Further details of the model validation and performance can be 
obtained in Methods in the Data Supplement.
RESUL TS
Baseline Characteristics
The mean follow-up of this large cohort of 3881 patients 
with FH was 8 years±9 years and ranged from <1 year 
to 48 years with a total of 32 361 person-years of follow-
up. Baseline characteristics of the participants according 
to the presence or absence of incident ASCVD events 
are presented in Table 1. A total of 383 incident ASCVD 
events occurred during follow-up, whereas 3498 patients 
remained free of ASCVD. Briefly, there was a higher fre-
quency of men in the ASCVD group, and these patients 
were older, with a significantly higher frequency of hyper-
tension, diabetes, and smoking. Body mass index, total 
cholesterol, triglycerides, LDL-C, non-HDL-C, apolipopro-
tein B, and lipoprotein (a) (Lp(a)) were also significantly 
higher in the ASCVD group compared with the non-
ASCVD group. Conversely, HDL-C was significantly lower 
in the ASCVD group. There was no difference between 
groups regarding the frequency of LL T use, presence of 
an FH-causing mutation, and definite FH according to the 
Dutch Lipid Clinic Network score (ie, >8 points).
Predictors of 10-Year ASCVD Risk
As shown in Table 2, all variables were significant predic-
tors of 10-year ASCVD risk in a univariate model. After 
a forward stepwise selection procedure, the predictors 
that remained in the multivariable model were age, sex, 
hypertension, active smoking, untreated LDL-C, HDL-C, 
and, Lp(a).
FH-Risk-Score Equation and Conversion to 
Scoring System
The HR and beta coefficient for each variable or categories 
of continuous variables included in the FH-Risk-Score are 
presented in Table II in the Data Supplement. The equation 
of the FH-Risk-Score using these beta coefficient values 
is also illustrated. The overall observed 10-year cohort risk 
using the Kaplan-Meier estimate is 11·1%. The FH-Risk-
Score distribution is illustrated in Figure I in the Data Sup-
plement. The average 10-year ASCVD risk predicted by the 
FH-Risk-Score equation is 11.4%, whereas the median risk 
is 8.2%. As an alternative to the use of the regression equa-
tion, a simple point chart derived from the beta coefficient 
values is proposed in Table 3. The 10-year ASCVD risk 
associated with each score value is presented in Table 4.
Internal Validation
The estimated optimism (or bias) obtained by the boot-
strap when using deciles of the FH-Risk-Score into the 
Cox regression model with 100 bootstrap resampling 
was equal to 0.002. The P for the association remained 
significant (P=0.010).
Performance: Survival Analysis, Discrimination, 
and Calibration
Kaplan-Meier event-free survival analysis according 
to the FH-Risk-Score groups (≤ versus > median of 
8.20%) are presented for 10-year total ASCVD (Figure 
[A]), 10-year MACE-3 (Figure [B]), and 30-year CV death 
(Figure [C]). The Kaplan-Meier analysis showed that the 
probability of event-free survival was significantly worse 
in the group with a higher baseline FH-Risk-Score for 
10-year ASCVD (0.819±0.012 versus 0.964±0.006, 
P<0.0001), 10-year MACE-3 (0.939±0.007 ver-
sus 0.985±0.004, P<0.0001), and 30-year CV death 
(0.909±0.028 versus 0.993±0.006, P<0.0001).
The association between the FH-Risk-Score group 
and CV outcomes was also analyzed using Cox regression 
models, as presented in Table 5. At 10 years of follow-
up, an ASCVD event occurred in 254 patients, including 
221 coronary artery disease events, 85 MACE-3, 196 
MACE-5, and 65 nonfatal MI. At 30 years, 23 CV deaths 
and 44 all-cause deaths occurred. A higher baseline FH-
Risk-Score was associated with a significant greater 
risk for 10-year ASCVD (HR, 5.52 [95% CI, 3.94–7 .73], 
P<0.0001), 10-year MACE-3 (HR, 4.64 [95% CI, 2.66–
8.11], P<0.0001), and 30-year CV death (HR, 10·73 
[95% CI, 2.51–45.79], P=0.0014). The association was 
also significant for 10-year coronary artery disease and 
MACE-5, 30-year all-cause mortality, and isolated ASCVD 
events: angina, unstable angina, MI, coronary revascular-
ization, and stroke (Table III in the Data Supplement).
The Harrell C-index for 10-year ASCVD was 0.75 for 
the FH-Risk-Score, which was significantly higher than both 
the Montreal-FH-SCORE (C-index=0.67) and the SAFE-
HEART-RE (C-index=0.69; P<0.0001 for both compari-
sons). A calibration plot that compares the predicted versus 
observed 10-year ASCVD risk by deciles of the FH-Risk-
Score is presented in Figure II in the Data Supplement. The 
overall predicted/observed ratio is equal to 1.0.
