Sensitivity Analysis
When the Cox analysis for 10-year total ASCVD event 
was divided per study cohort (Figure III in the Data Sup-
plement), the model was significant for all cohorts, except 
for the Robarts Research Institute (no cardiovascular 
event). The P for the interaction was 0·78. When divided 
by subgroups of clinical variables (Figure IV in the Data 
Supplement), the FH-Risk-Score was significantly asso-
ciated with incidence of 10-year total ASCVD events in 
all subgroups, except in diabetic subjects (HR, 1.72 [95% 
CI, 0.49–6.04], P=0.40). There was no significant inter-
action between subgroups. When subjects with an FH-
causing mutation were compared with subjects without 
an identified FH-causing mutation (negative [n=420] and 
not tested [n=578]), the HRs for the 10-year ASCVD 
risk remained significant in both groups (Table IVA and 
IVB and Figure VA and VB in the Data Supplement). 
When only the subjects who underwent genetic testing 
were studied, the HRs for the 10-year ASCVD risk also 
remained significant in both groups (data not shown).
DISCUSSION
In this study, the FH-Risk-Score was developed to pre-
dict the incidence of ASCVD events over a period of 10 
years in subjects with no prior cardiovascular event and 
is comprised of 7 simple clinical variables, namely sex, 
age, HDL-C, LDL-C, hypertension, smoking, and Lp(a). 
We demonstrated that the FH-Risk-Score was a strong 
predictor of 10-year ASCVD as well as 10-year MACE-3 
and 30-year CV death. This is the first study to demon-
strate a risk score that successfully predicts CV death 
in FH. Furthermore, this score is both applicable in FH 
subjects with and without a known FH-causing mutation. 
Therefore, the current study provides solid evidence for 
the clinical usefulness of the FH-Risk-Score.
ASCVD risk in FH is very high compared with the gen-
eral population due to the cumulative exposure to elevated 
LDL-C in circulation.8 In the last few decades, efforts have 
been made to develop strategies to drastically reduce 
LDL-C levels. In addition to statin trials, the IMPROVE-IT 
(Improved Reduction of Outcomes: Vytorin Efficacy Interna-
tional Trial), FOURIER (Further Cardiovascular Outcomes 
Research With PCSK9 Inhibition in Subjects With Elevated 
Risk), and ODYSSEY OUTCOMES (Evaluation of Cardio-
vascular Outcomes After an Acute Coronary Syndrome 
During Treatment With Alirocumab) trials of ezetimibe, 
evolocumab, and alirocumab, respectively, in the general 
population confirmed the lower is better approach for man-
aging LDL-C and cardiovascular risk, independently of the 
LDL-C reducing strategy used.32,33 The concept of younger 
is better in FH has also been demonstrated and clearly 
shows that early initiation of statin therapy during childhood 
is associated with a significant reduction in ASCVD risk 
compared with later initiation of therapy.9
