2636  October 2021 Arterioscler Thromb Vasc Biol. 2021;41:2632–2640. DOI: 10.1161/ATVBAHA.121.316106
Traditionally, LDL-C has always been the treatment tar-
get in patients with FH. Although the suitability of using 
additional lipid-lowering therapies in all patients with FH 
is questionable,34 there is no doubt that early aggressive 
LDL-C lowering through statin therapy remains the most 
important intervention for these high-risk patients. Results 
from our study showed that the median 10-year risk of 
ASCVD in patients with FH was 8.20%, but that this risk 
can be greatly variable in individual subjects ranging from 
0.58% to 1.96% (lowest decile of the score) to 25.54% 
to 81.87% (highest decile). Traditionally, 10-year ASCVD 
risk is considered low if <10%, moderate if between 
10, and 20%, and elevated if > 20%. In the present 
cohort using these cutoff values, 58% of patients were 
at low risk, 25% were at moderate risk, and 16% were 
at elevated risk. Furthermore, only 254 ASCVD events 
occurred at 10 years suggesting that statin treated FH 
subjects have an overall low risk of ASCVD although our 
score was able to identify higher risk subjects who could 
benefit from more aggressive risk reduction.
Indeed, it is time to start prioritizing risk factors other 
than just LDL-C in the treatment of patients with FH, as 
they play a crucial role in the refinement of cardiovascular 
risk stratification in FH.12,35 Other cardiovascular risk strati-
fication tools using similar variables have been proposed in 
FH. This includes the SAFEHEART-RE17 and the SIDIAP-
FHP36 (Sistema d’Informació per al Desenvolupament de 
l’Investigació en Atenció Primària) that were both devel-
oped in Spanish cohorts. However, the SAFEHEART-RE 
included patients with prevalent ASCVD in the equation. 
While interesting from a research perspective, it is less 
clinically relevant since secondary prevention patient 
are known to be at very high risk of ASCVD and already 
require aggressive therapy. In the present study, we com-
pared the performance indicators of the SAFEHEART-RE 
and the FH-Risk-Score. The FH-Risk-Score had a signifi-
cantly better discriminatory ability to predict total ASCVD 
events over 10 years. Furthermore, the calibration of the 
SAFEHEART-RE was suboptimal, with an overall pre-
dicted/observed ratio of 0·15 for 10-year ASCVD, which 
represents an under-estimation of the risk. The SIDIAP-
FHP has been developed and validated in patients having 
an FH phenotype according to LDL-C value solely, which 
is not a robust definition of FH. Indeed, even if their LDL-C 
cutoff for inclusion corresponds to possible, probable, or 
likely FH according to the Dutch Lipid Clinic Network 
criteria, the extremely high rates of hypertension (61% 
for patients in primary prevention and 93% for patients 
in secondary prevention) and diabetes (22%–37%) 
observed in this cohort is not concordant with what is typi-
cally observed in genetically confirmed patients with FH. 
This strongly suggests that this cohort is composed mostly 
of subjects having polygenic hypercholesterolemia. Com-
parison with the SIDIAP-FHP was not possible since the 
regression equation is not publicly available.
The FH-Risk-Score solely included patients with pri-
mary prevention FH between 18 and 65 years of age and 
for whom a genetic confirmation or a clinical diagnosis of 
FH by the Dutch Lipid Clinic Network (probable or defi-
nite FH) was available. The FH-Risk-Score was devel-
oped using a regression equation, but we also created a 
user-friendly chart points system derived from this equa-
tion that showed similar performance (data not shown).
The event rates observed in the present study (10-year 
ASCVD: 7%) are comparable to what has been observed 
in the SAFEHEART (nonfatal ASCVD: 5.1%) 17 and the 
