SIDIAP (ASCVD event: 12%)36 cohorts, considering that 
the follow-up duration was shorter in these cohorts (mean 
of 5.5 years and median of 5.0 years, respectively, versus 
mean of 8 years in the present study) and that the age 
at baseline was drastically higher in the SIDIAP cohort. 
The incidence of CHD death was very high in the Simon 
Broome cohort (7%),37 but the follow-up is much longer 
(median of 20 years and up to 36 years). In addition, 
these cohorts included patients in secondary prevention, 
which are at increased risk of recurrent event and death.37
One clinical application of the FH-Risk-Score could be 
to modulate treatment according to risk in primary pre-
vention. Of course, the first step of pharmacological treat-
ment remains the optimization of statin treatments in all 
FH subjects, regardless of the predicted 10-year ASCVD 
risk. Also, the treatment should be initiated as young as 
8 or 10 years of age, as chronic exposure to elevated 
LDL-C is the most important risk factor for ASCVD in 
patients with FH.9 As the second step, clinicians should 
assess and treat other ASCVD risk factors such as 
hypertension, diabetes, and smoking through lifestyle 
changes or appropriate pharmaceutical treatments. As 
the third step, patients with a moderate (10-year risk of 
10%–20%) to high (10-year risk > 20%) risk of incident 
ASCVD events could be prioritized to receive additional 
lipid-lowering therapy such as ezetimibe and or PCSK9 
