the majority of the cohort (74%) had a genetically con-
firmed diagnosis of FH but the FH-Risk-Score was both 
predictive in clinically diagnosed FH as well as in geneti-
cally confirmed FH subjects, which reflects the reality of 
clinical diagnosis of FH in most of the world. Furthermore, 
this study represents a large, prospective and multinational 
cohort from Europe and North America with an extensive 
follow-up of >32 361 person-years. Another major strength 
is the adherence of this study to the TRIPOD guideline.
The limitations of the present study include the rela-
tively modest number of CV deaths that occurred during 
the follow-up. Furthermore, it appears that the FH-Risk-
Score does not predict ASCVD events as effectively in 
diabetic subjects. This could be due to the low prevalence 
of diabetes in our FH cohort as was previously pub-
lished.38 Since diabetes is a cardiovascular risk equivalent 
in the general population, it would be reasonable to con-
sider diabetic FH subjects to have a de facto high risk of 
ASCVD. In addition, details about the baseline LL T was 
not available in the UK Biobank cohort, which affected 
the imputation of baseline LDL-C, total cholesterol, and 
non-HDL-C. Furthermore, patients included in our cohort 
are mainly of white origin, although the precise propor-
tion is not known. An external validation in different ethnic 
groups would be required. Also, the lack of a polygenic 
risk score for coronary artery disease or for LDL-C to fur-
ther refine the risk stratification is also a limitation of the 
present study. Finally, the change in LL T and the cumula-
tive LDL-C exposure over the time of the follow-up as 
well as detailed information about previous LL T was not 
documented but would have been a covariable of interest.
CONCLUSIONS
This study investigated the prediction of cardiovascular 
events using the FH-Risk-Score in a large prospective 
multinational cohort of adult patients with FH. We have 
shown that this score performs well to predict the 10-year 
ASCVD risk in patients with FH using simple clinical vari-
ables: sex, age, HDL-C, untreated LDL-C, hypertension, 
smoking, and Lp(a). Importantly, this is the first score 
that successfully predicts CV death in FH. The FH-Risk-
Score could therefore be used to guide the intensity of 
the LL T treatment in FH subjects. Furthermore, this study 
strengthens the concept of risk heterogeneity in patients 
with FH. In the era of precision medicine, it is important to 
consider cardiovascular risk factors other than LDL-C in 
the treatment of patients with FH, in light of their impor-
tant role in the cardiovascular risk in this population.
