SUPPLEMENTAL METHODS  1 
Study population and outcomes 2 
The Montreal Clinical Research Institute (IRCM) familial hypercholesterolemia 3 
(FH) cohort 4 
Patient included in the IRCM FH cohort have been recruited at the Lipid Clinic of 5 
the IRCM, Montreal, Canada. All coronary artery disease (CAD), peripheral 6 
vascular disease (PVD), and cerebrovascular disease (CeVD) events were 7 
objectively confirmed and included in the analysis. This included obtaining notes 8 
from admission, cardiology consultations, and testing such as coronary 9 
angiogram and non-invasive tests. The events were: angina, unstable angina, 10 
myocardial infarction (MI), coronary angioplasty, coronary artery bypass graft, 11 
claudication, peripheral angioplasty, peripheral arterial surgery, transient 12 
ischemic attack, stroke, carotid endarterectomy, cardiovascular death, and all-13 
cause mortality. Data for the baseline lipid profiles were collected without any 14 
Lipid-lowering therapy (LLT) and measured by lipoprotein ultracentrifugation. The 15 
presence of a classical French-Canadian FH-causing mutation in the LDLR gene 16 
(>15kb deletion, >5kb deletion, W87G (exon 3), E228K (exon 4), and C667Y 17 
(exon 14) was assessed as described previously.19 Baseline was defined as the 18 
first medical visit at the IRCM lipid clinic, whereas end of the follow-up was fixed 19 
at the last medical visit that was documented in the medical file or in 2016 for 20 
patients who were still followed at the lipid clinic. The duration of the medical 21 
follow-up varied from < 1 year and up to 48 years (mean: 13 years ± 11 years). 22 
 23 
The British Columbia FH Registry 24 
Patients included in the British Columbia FH Registry have been recruited at the 25 
Healthy Heart Program Prevention clinic at St. Paul’s Hospital, Vancouver, 26 
Canada. Incident cardiovascular events that were available in this registry 27 
included coronary events (angina, unstable angina, coronary artery bypass graft, 28 
and MI) as well as cardiovascular death and all-cause mortality. Clinical baseline 29 
and lipid profile data were collected from the patients’ medical records at the first 30 
visit at the lipid clinic. Lipid profiles were measured in core laboratories of the 31 
public health systems of Canada, which use standardized methods. Baseline 32 
LDL-C, total cholesterol, and non-HDL-C were without LLT or imputed.20 End of 33 
the follow-up was fixed at the last medical visit or in 2019 for patients that are still 34 
followed at the lipid clinic. The duration of the follow-up varied between <1 and 35 
34 years (mean: 10 years ± 9 years). Details concerning the British Columbia FH 36 
Registry and the genetic screening method in this cohort have been published 37 
elsewhere.6,21 38 
 39 
The UK Biobank 40 
=== PDF PAGE 3 ===
 3 
 
The UK Biobank is an open-access database.22 This prospective cohort study 1 
includes 500,000 participants recruited in 22 different centers in United Kingdom 2 
from 2006 to 2010 (baseline date). For the present study, the end of follow-up 3 
was fixed in June 2020 for all participants except those who were lost during the 4 
follow-up or deceased. The incident events documented in this database 5 
included angina, unstable angina, coronary revascularization, MI, peripheral 6 
revascularization, transient ischemic attack, carotid revascularization, and stroke, 7 
as well as cardiovascular death and all-cause mortality. Baseline lipid profile was 8 
with or without LLT. However details concerning the treatment regimen and 9 
dosages were not available. For patients taking lipid-lowering therapy, baseline 10 
LDL-C, total cholesterol, and non-HDL-C values were imputed using a fixed 11 
expected percent reduction of 30%.23 Genotyping was performed for the whole 12 
cohort and exome sequencing was performed on ~50 thousand subjects.24 We 13 
extracted from the UK Biobank all individuals for whom a putative FH-causing 14 
variant in the LDLR, APOB, or PCSK9 genes was identified. Frameshift, 15 
nonsense, or canonical splice site variants were considered pathogenic if 16 
annotated 'pathogenic' or 'likely pathogenic' in ClinVar. The missense LDLR 17 
variants were considered pathogenic if deemed damaging by at least 5 of 6 18 
bioinformatic prediction tools.25,26 A more exhaustive description of the UK 19 
Biobank has been provided elsewhere.22 The duration of the follow-up varied 20 
between <1 and 13 years for all subjects included in this study (mean: 11 years ± 21 
1 year).  22 
 23 
The Robarts Research Institute 24 
Patients from the Robarts Research Institute were recruited between 1998 and 25 
2020. Incident atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) that were 26 
documented in these patients included angina, unstable angina, coronary 27 
angioplasty, coronary artery bypass graft, MI, and stroke. However, no event 28 
occurred during the follow-up. All individuals were genotyped with validated 29 
targeted next-generation sequencing panel.27 Clinical baseline and lipid profile 30 
data were collected from the patients’ medical records at the first visit at the 31 
Institute. Lipid profiles were determined after a 12 to 14 hour fast using the 32 
COBAS B101 system (Roche Diagnostics, Mississauga, ON). Baseline LDL-C, 33 
total cholesterol, and non-HDL-C were without lipid-lowering treatment or 34 
imputed.20 The follow-up duration varied from < 1 year to 22 years (mean: 5 35 
years ± 5 years), with last year of follow-up in 2020.  36 
 37 
The REFERCHOL Registry 38 
Patients included in the national French Register of FH (REFERCHOL) have 39 
been recruited in twenty different sites across the country. The register was 40 
established in 2015 by the New French Society of Atherosclerosis. However, for 41 
patients who were already followed at one of these different sites, retrospective 42 
data extraction from the patients' medical records was done. More extensive 43 
=== PDF PAGE 4 ===
 4 
 
details about this register have been published previously.4 Most of patients had 1 
a genetic screening for classical genes involved in FH (APOB, LDLR, and 2 
PCSK9).28 Assessment of biological parameters, including a plasma lipid profile, 3 
were performed in the clinical laboratory attached to each lipid clinic, using assay 4 
methods subject to quality control. Baseline LDL-C, total cholesterol, and non-5 
HDL-C were without lipid-lowering treatment or imputed.20 Events included MI, 6 
angina, unstable angina (defined according to the European Society of 7 
Cardiology/American College of Cardiology)29, coronary angioplasty, coronary 8 
artery bypass graft, stroke, carotid endarterectomy, carotid angioplasty, 9 
peripheral angioplasty, peripheral arterial surgery, cardiovascular death, and all-10 
cause mortality. Follow-up duration was defined as the time from the enrollment 11 
visit (or the first medical visit for the retrospective data extraction) to the latest 12 
medical visit. The follow-up duration varied between < 1 year and 39 years, with 13 
a mean length of 7 ± 9 years.  14 
 15 
Statistical analyses 16 
Continuous variables with skewed distribution were log-transformed prior 17 
baseline comparison between groups. Normally distributed variables are 18 
expressed as means ± standard deviation (SD), whereas abnormally distributed 19 
variables are expressed as median (IQR). Comparisons between groups for 20 
baseline characteristics were calculated using Chi2 or Student’s t-Test. In order to 21 
identify predictors of 10-year ASCVD risk, hazard ratios (HRs) and 95% 22 
confidence intervals (CIs) were generated using Cox proportional hazards 23 
models. The significant univariate predictors were entered into the multivariable 24 
model by forward stepwise selection. 25 
 26 
Missing values 27 
All 3881 individuals had complete information on age, HDL-C, sex, hypertension, 28 
smoking, Dutch Lipid Clinic Network (DLCN) score, as well as variables related 29 
to the follow-up duration and time-to-event. Missing values for variables of the 30 
baseline characteristics were not imputed (<2% missing values for LDL-C, total 31 
cholesterol, non-HDL-C, triglycerides, diabetes, active smoking status, and 32 
history of LLT use, 8% for body mass index (BMI), 29% for lipoprotein (a) (Lp(a)), 33 
and 40% for apolipoprotein B). However, because Lp(a) group was found to be a 34 
significant predictor of the 10-year ASCVD risk in the multivariable analysis and 35 
included in the model, missing values for this variable were further imputed using 36 
the median (20·7 mg/dL).  37 
 38 
Internal validation 39 
The internal validation of the score was performed by bootstrapping method, 40 
using 100 bootstrap samples and a simple sampling method. Indeed, according 41 
to the TRIPOD guidelines, splitting the dataset into development and validation 42 
=== PDF PAGE 5 ===
 5 
 
data sets is a weak and inefficient form of internal validation and does not 1 
represent external validation. Bootstrapping or cross-validation are the 2 
recommended methods for internal validation.31 3 
 4 
Performance: Survival analysis, discrimination, and calibration 5 
Kaplan-Meier event-free survival analysis according to the FH-Risk-Score group 6 
(above and below the median of 8·20% 10-year risk of ASCVD events) were 7 
generated with P values obtained from the non-parametric log-rank test. HR from 8 
Cox regression model has also been generated for each outcome and outcome 9 
groups according to the FH-Risk-Score group. For these analyses, the 10 
significance level was adjusted by the Bonferroni correction for the primary and 11 
secondary main outcomes (P<0·017). There were no violations of the 12 
proportional hazard assumption for the Cox analyses for all events categories, 13 
except for 10-year stroke. The performance of the FH-Risk-Score was assessed 14 
by discrimination and calibration methods. The discrimination of the FH-Risk-15 
Score to predict different outcomes as well as performance comparison with 16 
other published scores were assessed by Harrell’s C-index for survival data. The 17 
Montreal-FH-SCORE and the SAFEHEART-RE were calculated as published 18 
previously.15,16,17 A calibration plot was generated to compare the predicted 10-19 
year ASCVD risk using the FH-Risk-Score equation and the observed 10-year 20 
ASCVD risk using the Kaplan-Meier estimate. The calibration plot was illustrated 21 
by deciles of the FH-Risk-Score. The overall predicted/observed ratio is 22 
presented.  23 
 24 
Sensitivity Analyses 25 
Forrest plots have been generated to illustrate the 10-year HR for any ASCVD 26 
events according to the FH-Risk-Score group for each individual cohort and by 27 
subgroups using Cox regression.  28 
29 
