./debate/steps/step-000/r1/panel.json:29:      "response": "• I'll read the step brief first, then audit the permitted resources.\n\n• I'll check which permitted resources exist, then read them in parallel.\n\n• Now the README, the main script, and locating the CALON_JULIUS_SPECKIT resources.\n\n• The Julius spec-kit isn't in this repo. Let me check the parent CALON directory, since the brief permits it.\n\n• Now the Julius spec-kit resources.\n\n• I've read the core repo resources. Now the spec-kit research/checklist files, its outputs, and a check for comparator source PDFs on disk.\n\n• The spec-kit FH-RS chart matches PDF Table 3 exactly. Now confirm SAFEHEART's published Table 3 hazard ratios against the code/15 version.\n\n• SAFEHEART's published Table 3 matches the spec-kit, not code/15. Checking FH-RS Table 4 risk bands and looking for a Montreal PDF anywhere on disk.\n\n• One verification before writing: do the spec-kit comparator tests actually pass on this machine?\n\n• # Step 000, Round 1 — Resource audit (kimi, independent and blind)\n\n  Everything below was produced from the permitted resources only. I did not open any participant-level file. All counts cited are aggregate and already present in the admitted documents.\n\n  ## Resources examined (ledger)\n\n  | Resource | Path | What it establishes | What it does not establish |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | Data inventory | `debate/DATA-INVENTORY.md` | File shapes, traps, derivation rules, reference counts (risk set **3,209** after 207 prevalent + 124 undated excluded) | Whether any script actually implements those rules |\n  | Mandates | `debate/mandates/{AGENT,ORCHESTRATOR,ROLES}.md` | Protocol; full-matrix design; governance | — |\n  | State | `debate/state/{open-questions,transcript,locked-decisions}.md` | OBJ-001/002/003 open; step 001 not yet started | — |\n  | Scripts | `debate/bin/{panel,consensus}.mjs` | Closure mechanics | — |\n  | Step-002 brief | `debate/steps/step-002-QC-BRIEF.md` | The disputed numbers, as prose | No artefact behind the Julius numbers |\n  | Locked pipeline | `code/15_CALON_FINAL.py`, `outputs/calon_final.json`, `outputs/calon_final_qc.json`, `STATUS.md`, `README.md` | What the local model is and does | Comparator provenance |\n  | Spec-kit | `CALON_JULIUS_SPECKIT/scripts/comparators.py`, `tests/test_comparators.py`, `specs/001-calon-fh-5y/research.md`, `checklists/comparator-fidelity.md` | A PDF-cited comparator library; all 6 tests pass on this machine (ran `python3 -m unittest tests.test_comparators` — OK) | Whether its equations were ever the ones scored in the disputed Julius run |\n  | Source PDFs | `/Users/nader85/Documents/CALON/thesis_60000_consensus/sources/SAFEHEART_2017.pdf`, `FH_Risk_Score_2021.pdf` | The published SAFEHEART-RE equation (Table 3 + two worked examples) and the FH-RS chart (Tables 3–4) | FH-RS regression betas (in the Data Supplement, **not on disk**); Montreal anything (**no Montreal PDF on disk** — searched for montreal/paquette/jacl, none found) |\n\n  ---\n\n  ## 1. METHODOLOGY\n\n  **The estimand in the admitted evidence is not stated, and the two sides are probably not estimating the same thing.** `code/15_CALON_FINAL.py` computes Harrell's C over full follow-up (median 14.6 y UKB) via `concordance_index` on cross-validated partial hazards (`cv()`, lines 312–351). The step-002 brief's disputed WINs are framed at a **10-year horizon** (\"Julius declares two wins at the 10-year horizon\", §C). Table B then puts \"local pipeline 0.6944\" and \"Julius 0.628\" in one row labelled only \"C\". A full-follow-up Harrell C and a 10-year horizon C are different estimands with different expected values; if that is what the table mixes, part of the −0.06 SAFEHEART gap could be estimand mismatch rather than scoring error. Nothing in the admitted set states which C each number is. This must be resolved before OBJ-001's substitution arithmetic means anything.\n\n  **The cohort definition in the locked output contradicts the admitted reference counts — arithmetically, not approximately.** `outputs/calon_final.json` reports UKB n=**3,333**. `debate/DATA-INVENTORY.md` (reference-count table and derivation rules) and `debate/steps/step-002-QC-BRIEF.md` Table A both state the risk set is **3,209**, after excluding 207 prevalent *and* 124 undated atherosclerosis-positive participants (\"Undated case — atherosclerosis-positive, no usable date → **exclude, not a non-case**\"). 3,540 − 207 = 3,333 exactly: the locked output excluded the prevalent and stopped. And `code/15_CALON_FINAL.py:176-203` confirms the mechanism — `prevalent` requires `ev.notna()`, so an athero-flagged participant with no usable event date falls through to `E=0` and is **retained as a non-case**, the exact misclassification the inventory rule forbids. Treating ~124 atherosclerosis-positive people as event-free biases both discrimination and every head-to-head cell. The brief's Table A lists 3,209 under \"numbers that AGREE across both analyses\" — that agreement claim is false against the local pipeline's own locked artefact. Filed as OBJ-004 below.\n\n  **Collinearity guard cannot see the collinearity OBJ-003 is about.** `usable()` (code/15, lines 300–301) applies the |r|≥0.999 drop rule only to features whose name starts with `\"sp\"`. OBJ-003 concerns age vs cholesterol-years; `cum_nonhdl` is not an `sp*` feature, so the guard is structurally incapable of firing on it even at r=1.0. The \"check\" exists and reports `dropped_collinearity_guard: [\"sp18\",\"sp30\"]` in `calon_final.json` — it looks like the specified check ran; it cannot detect the specified problem. Additionally, OBJ-003's claim describes cholesterol-years as \"age × untreated total cholesterol\", but the shipped definition is `log(nonhdl_unt × age)` (line 249–250). The objection's premise needs restating against the shipped variable before its `settled_by` refit is interpretable.\n\n  ## 2. IMPLEMENTATION\n\n  **The local SAFEHEART is verifiably not SAFEHEART-RE.** I extracted `/Users/nader85/Documents/CALON/thesis_60000_consensus/sources/SAFEHEART_2017.pdf` and read Table 3 and the worked examples (pp. 2138–2139 of the article). The published LP is categorical: 0.70·male + 1.07·age30–59 + 1.45·age≥60 + 0.69·HBP + 1.42·priorASCVD + 0.48·smoking + 0.88·overweight + 0.98·obesity + 0.92·LDL100–159 + 1.57·LDL≥160 + 0.42·Lp(a)>50mg/dL, S₀(5)=0.9532, centring 5.4078, with worked examples 0.0002148 and 0.3808. `CALON_JULIUS_SPECKIT/scripts/comparators.py:27-74` implements exactly this and its tests reproduce both worked examples (tests pass, verified by running them). `code/15_CALON_FINAL.py:278-279` instead scores `0.045·age + 0.6·male + 0.4·htn + 0.3·smoke + 0.02·bmi + 0.15·ldl + 0.25·lpa_hi` — continuous where the publication is categorical, no prior-ASCVD term, and not one coefficient equals ln of any published HR (male: published β=0.698, code has 0.6; HBP: 0.688 vs 0.4; smoking: 0.482 vs 0.3; Lp(a): 0.419 vs 0.25). This is not a monotone reparametrisation; the ranking differs. **Every SAFEHEART cell in `outputs/calon_final.json`** (comparator_c 0.6944 UKB ALL, 0.7259 Wales ALL, and 26 subgroup comparisons) is scored against a function that is not the published equation, and no citation in the file says where those weights came from. This is the same defect shape as the withdrawn `code/08` comparators documented in `STATUS.md` (\"invented weights … docstring claimed published provenance\"). Filed as OBJ-005.\n\n  **FH-RS and Montreal in code/15 are unverifiable from anything on disk.** The FH-RS PDF's main text verifies only the point chart (Tables 3–4 — which match `fhrs_chart_points`/`fhrs_chart_risk_percent` in the spec-kit exactly, including the 53–55 → \">75%\" band); the regression betas live in Table II of the **Data Supplement, which is not on disk**. code/15's FH-RS weights (age bands 0.938…2.256, male 0.721, htn 0.644, smoke 0.625, lpa 0.434) therefore cannot be checked against a source, and one internal signal is adverse: main-text Table 2 gives male HR 1.82 (β=0.599), not 0.721. code/15's \"Montreal\" (0.75·z(age) − 0.27·z(HDL) + 0.25·male + 0.19·htn + 0.12·smoke) is a standardized-beta form of a score published as integer points, and **no Montreal/Combined-FH PDF exists on disk** (searched by filename across `~/Documents/CALON`). The brief's reassurance that \"Montreal reproduces to 0.003\" only shows both pipelines scored the *same* function; it says nothing about whether that function is Paquette 2017. Filed as OBJ-006.\n\n  **The local comparator code violates the project's own admitted fidelity checklist in at least four places.** Against `checklists/comparator-fidelity.md`: COMP-06 — prior-ASCVD term absent rather than fixed-to-zero-with-label; COMP-07 — FH-RS applied with no 18–65 eligibility restriction; COMP-10 — missing inputs silently substituted rather than returning NOT_EVALUABLE: `hdl.fillna(1.35)`, `ldl.fillna(median)`, `bmi.fillna(median or 27.0)`, and `lpa.fillna(0)` so the 22.5% UKB Lp(a)-missing are silently scored low-Lp(a), and in Wales `x[\"lpa\"] = np.nan` (line 232) silently strips the Lp(a) term from both scores for the entire cohort; COMP-05 — the 105 threshold on `lpa_chem` (nmol/L) against a published ≥50 mg/dL cut-point is exactly the fixed universal mass↔molar conversion research.md forbids (\"Never convert Lp(a) from nmol/L using a universal factor\").\n\n  **The QC addendum audits a model that was withdrawn.** `outputs/calon_final_qc.json` (product of `code/15b_QC_ADDENDUM.py`) lists features including `hdl` and `bmi` — the terms removed on 13 August for outcome-informed selection (`STATUS.md`, code/15 lines 77–102). Its C (0.69956) is the pre-removal model's; the locked model's is 0.69971. The calibration slope 1.214 [1.017, 1.411] quoted in `STATUS.md` as a limitation of CALON-F therefore describes the **superseded 10-term fit**, not the locked 8-term one. If the locked model's slope differs, the manuscript's stated limitation is about a model that no longer exists. Folded into OBJ-007.\n\n  **Stale README.** `README.md` still ships the withdrawn model: the spec line includes HDL-C; \"the two losses are both in UK Biobank participants with diabetes (79 events, C=0.536)\" vs the locked tally of **one** loss with 64 events and C=0.5593; Welsh \"8.4 events per variable\" vs locked EPV 11.5; UKB person-years 45,451 vs locked 45,850. Nothing tests docs against outputs, so nothing failed. Filed as OBJ-007.\n\n  **Machinery notes (checked, held):** `consensus.mjs` parses and enforces what it claims; I verified the schema rules against the register, and OBJ-000/OBJ-001–003 are well-formed. Two procedural facts matter for this step: (a) step 000 currently has objections from only one agent (OBJ-000, kimi), so the two-agent rule (`consensus.mjs:128-129`) makes step 000 unclosable until at least one other agent files a step-000 objection; (b) `panel.mjs`'s PATH fix (lines 28–38) is the documented guard against silent 5→4 panel shrinkage — good — but `have()` failure still degrades the panel silently to whoever is installed, with only the summary line to notice.\n\n  **OBJ-002's central quantity exists in no admitted artefact.** The 0.52–0.57 comparator-slope cluster appears only as prose in the step-002 brief and OBJ-002. Neither `calon_final.json` nor `calon_final_qc.json` reports comparator slopes (the QC file's slopes, 1.214/1.257, are the model's own). `CALON_JULIUS_SPECKIT/outputs/` contains only `consistency_report.json`, `preflight_report.json`, `spec_manifest.json` — no analysis results. The Julius run's numbers have no recomputable artefact in the admitted set at all. Filed as OBJ-008.\n\n  ## 3. ADVERSARIAL AUDIT\n\n  Assume the strongest claim — \"the local pipeline is the trustworthy side; substituting its comparator values correctly turns both Julius WINs into TIEs\" — is wrong. The most likely way it is wrong: **the local comparator values are themselves artefacts of non-published equations**, so the substitution replaces one unverifiable number with another, and both the Julius WINs and the local TIEs are noise around mislabelled functions. What I found above makes this the leading hypothesis, not a hypothetical: the local \"SAFEHEART\" is falsified by the PDF on disk; the local \"FH-RS\" and \"Montreal\" have no checkable source. Note the asymmetry argument in OBJ-001 (\"every comparator Julius scores lower is one where a WIN was declared\") cuts both ways once neither side's implementation is anchored: the local pipeline scores SAFEHEART *higher* (0.6944) with an equation that is not SAFEHEART — a continuous-age, continuous-LDL, no-prior-ASCVD function is plausibly *better* matched to this cohort's incident design, which would inflate the comparator's apparent C and shrink the model's margin. The direction of the bias is not knowable without rescoring; that is the point.\n\n  Second attack: assume the locked model's 10/58/1 tally is right. What would have failed if the 124 undated athero-positive participants-as-non-cases changed any verdict? Nothing — no check in `code/15` counts them, no output names them, and the brief's Table A asserts agreement on 3,209 that the locked JSON contradicts. The defect is invisible to every guard in the pipeline. `outcome_provenance()` (lines 375–388) records the outcome *file* but never reconciles the cohort *n* against the inventory's reference counts — the one check that would have caught this was never written, despite the inventory saying \"your code must reproduce these.\"\n\n  What held under attack: the spec-kit comparator library. I tried to falsify it against the PDFs and could not — SAFEHEART matches Table 3 and both worked examples; the FH-RS chart matches Tables 3–4 band-for-band; its six tests pass on this machine. Also held: the endpoint correction arithmetic (62 of 351 HF-only, consistent across STATUS.md, code/15 docstring, and DATA-INVENTORY trap 3) and the Welsh counts (1,159/92 in both the inventory and the locked JSON).\n\n  ## 4. CLINICAL AND PUBLICATION REALITY\n\n  A lipidologist's first question about any FH risk-model paper is \"which equation, exactly, did you score the comparators with?\" On the admitted evidence this project currently cannot answer that question for the local pipeline: one comparator is demonstrably not the published equation, two have no source on disk, and the fidelity checklist the project itself wrote (COMP-05/06/07/10) is violated by the code that produced the headline tally. A hostile reviewer opens there, and does not need to read further: if SAFEHEART-RE is not SAFEHEART-RE, all 26 SAFEHEART cells and the \"10 wins\" framing are unquotable. Second opening: the n. A reviewer who reconciles the cohort flow (3,540 → −207 → −124 → 3,209, per the project's own inventory) against the model's n=3,333 will conclude that known atherosclerotic cases were analysed as event-free controls — a case/control misclassification that inflates everyone-years and can move C in either direction. Third: the one number quoted as the model's calibration honesty (slope 1.214, interval excluding 1) belongs to a feature set the authors themselves withdrew as outcome-informed; quoting it for the locked model is a citation of a model that does not exist. Nothing here changes practice, and nothing should be submitted until the comparators are re-scored from PDF-verified equations on the corrected risk set — the machinery to do SAFEHEART and the FH-RS chart correctly already exists and passes tests in `CALON_JULIUS_SPECKIT/scripts/comparators.py`.\n\n  ## Evidence still needed before OBJ-001 / OBJ-002 / OBJ-003 can close\n\n  - **OBJ-001** — (a) the Julius run's frozen extract and scoring code, or its raw output, deposited as a named artefact (today those numbers exist only as prose in the step-002 brief); (b) a",

