event in FH aged between 20 and 39 years and treated with
lipid-lowering therapy was greatly increased compared to
the expected frequency in the general population (48-fold
increase in men and 125-fold increase in women).
9 The
odds ratio for coronary artery disease was also increased
in genetically conﬁrmed FH patients (17-fold increased)
compared to normolipidemic individuals. For identical
LDL-C concentration, subjects with an identiﬁed FH-
causing mutation presented a 5-fold increase in CVD risk
compared to non-FH hypercholesterolemic subjects.
10
Although all FH individuals are at high risk of CVD due
to lifelong burden of LDL cholesterol accumulation, not all
these patients will develop premature atherosclerosis or
experience CVD events. Large kindred studies have shown
that in certain cases 40% of untreated FH patients had a
normal life expectancy.
11 Even in FH patients, age of onset
of CVD differs because of other environmental and genetic
factors that modulate the phenotypic expression and the
progression of the disease.
12,13 Therefore, these additional
risk factors could help to stratify CVD risk in FH and
should guide the treatment intensity.
14
In the general population, scores such as the Framing-
ham Risk Score help to stratify the risk of CVD. The
predicting variables include age, HDL-C, total cholesterol,
systolic blood pressure, smoking, diabetes, and family
history of premature CVD.
15 However, the Framingham
Risk Score and other risk calculators such as the Pooled
Cohort Equation
16,17 and the European SCORE 18 are not
designed to be applied in FH subjects. 14,19 Several risk fac-
tors, including age,20,21 male gender,21–23 hypertension,20,22
smoking,22,24 diabetes,22,24 high triglycerides level, 25 or
low HDL-C level 20,22,24 and high lipoprotein(a) (Lp(a))
level22–24 have independently been associated with CVD
risk in FH patients (summarized by Nordestgaard et al.,
2013).
3 However, the importance of each predictor is not
precisely established, and no risk calculator is available
in this population to stratify the CVD risk.
Considering that identifying and appropriately treating
FH subjects has been shown to be a highly cost-effective
strategy,
26 further stratiﬁcation of the cardiovascular risk in
FH patients could be useful. Therefore, the aims of our
study are twofold: to identify the strongest predictors of
CVD in our cohort of patients with FH and to develop a
practical risk-scoring tool to improve CVD risk stratiﬁca-
tion in FH patients.
Material and Methods
Study population and data collection
The data for this cross-sectional cohort study come from
the Nutrition, Metabolism and Atherosclerosis Clinic
(CNMA) of Institut de recherches cliniques de Montr /C19eal
(IRCM) database.
We screened 20,434 patients followed at the IRCM lipid
clinic to identify 2654 patients presenting the clinical
phenotype of FH (13% of patients) according to the Dutch
Lipid Criteria. All patients with a Dutch Lipid Criteria
score $3 (possible, probable or deﬁnite FH) were eligible
for FH-causing mutation screening to obtain a maximum of
sensitivity. Approximately half (1 267) of these potential
FH patients consented to provide DNA for genetic testing.
We identiﬁed 670 conﬁrmed adult FH patients who
presented a known FH-causing mutation in the LDLR
gene, including del .15 kb of the promoter and exon 1,
del .5 kb of exons 2 and 3, W66 G (exon 3), E207 K
(exon 4), Y468X (exon 10), and C646Y (exon 14). Clinical
data were obtained from the CNMA database and included
coronary, peripheral, and cerebrovascular events (angina,
myocardial infarction, coronary angioplasty, coronary
bypass surgery, claudication, peripheral angioplasty, pe-
ripheral arterial surgery, transient ischemic attack, stroke,
and carotid endarterectomy), characteristics such as gender,
age, hypertension, diabetes, past and current smoking
habits, body mass index (BMI), prior statin use, and lipid
proﬁle. Data for the lipid proﬁle were collected without
any lipid-lowering medication. Indeed, patients were asked
to stop any lipid-lowering medications for a period of
4 weeks before the blood draw. Diagnosis of hypertension
was established according to Canadian Hypertension Edu-
cation Program criteria, whereas diagnosis of diabetes
was made using the Canadian Diabetes Association guide-
lines. The most recent version of these criteria that was
available at the time of patient evaluation was used. Written
informed consent, approved by the IRCM institutional re-
view board and ethical committees, was obtained from all
the participants after reading a detailed consent form.
Biochemical analyses
Methods for DNA analysis and lipid and lipoprotein
determination have previously been described elsewhere. 12
Brieﬂy, the presence of W66 G (exon 3), E207 K (exon 4),
Y468X (exon 10), and C646Y (exon 14) mutations was
determined through a method based on the polymerase
chain reaction, whereas deletions .15 kb and . 5k b
were identiﬁed by Southern Blot or by semi-quantitative
Paquette et al A new score to predict CVD risk in FH 81
=== PDF PAGE 3 ===
 polymerase chain reaction assay. Plasma cholesterol and
triglyceride concentrations were measured through an
enzymatic method (Abbott Biochromatic Analyzer model
100, Abbott Laboratories, Pasadena, CA), whereas plasma
LDL-C, VLDL-C, and HDL-C concentrations were deter-
mined by ultracentrifugation.
27 Lp(a) concentration was
measured with a commercial ELISA kit (Macra EIA Kit,
Strategic Diagnostics Industries, Inc., Newark). Blood sam-
ples were drawn after a 12-h overnight fast.
Statistical analyses
Statistical analyses were performed using SAS 9.4 (SAS
Institute, Cary, NC) and SPSS 20.0 (IBM Corp, Armonk,
NY). A statistically signiﬁcant level of P , .05 was used
for all tests. Data for continuous normally distributed vari-
ables are expressed as mean 6 SD. Continuous logarithmic
variables are expressed as median (interquartile range).
Categorical variables are expressed as frequency (%). Uni-
variate logistic regression was performed to identify signif-
icant CVD predictors and the % of the variance explained
by each of these independent variables.
To assess independent associations between these vari-
ables and CVD events, all the univariate signiﬁcant pre-
dictors were entered into the multivariate model
sequentially, in descending order of b-coefﬁcients values,
by a stepwise method with a cut point of 0.05. For highly
correlated variables (eg, HDL-C and TG), we solely
considered the most predictive one for entry into the model
to avoid multicollinearity. We developed a CVD score, the
Montreal-FH-SCORE (MFHS) based on the multivariate
b-coefﬁcients for each of the independent predictors. For
the three categorical variables (gender, hypertension and
smoking), the unfavorable proﬁle (eg, presence of hyper-
tension) was associated with increased points proportional
to the b-coefﬁcient, whereas the favorable proﬁle was
associated with a score of 0. For continuous variables, each
increase (age) or decrease (HDL-C) of one standard
deviation (1 SD) was associated with additional units of
score according to the b-coefﬁcient.
The overall predictive ability of the Montreal-FH-
SCORE was quantiﬁed by computing the receiver operating
characteristic (ROC) curves (or C-statistic). MedCalc
(MedCalc Software, Mariakerke, Belgium) was used to
test the statistical signiﬁcance of the difference between
dependent ROC curves according to the method described
by Delong et al. (1988).
28 A chi-square test or Fisher exact
test was used to compare the frequency of CVD between
groups for each independent predictor.
All authors had full access to all the data in the study and
take responsibility for its integrity and the data analysis.
Results
We screened 20,434 patients with dyslipidemia to
identify carriers of an FH-causing mutation. A total of
670 adult Caucasian subjects with a known mutation in the
LDLR gene were included in the present study ( Fig. 1). The
15 kb deletion of the promoter and exon 1 of the LDLR
were the most frequent mutation observed in our cohort
(69.1%) followed by the W66 G mutation (17.8%). The
E207 K (2.7%), Y468X (0.1%), C646Y (8.4%) and the 5
kb deletion of exons 2 and 3 (2.2%) accounted for the bal-
ance of FH-causing mutations ( Supplemental Figure I ).
The characteristics of FH subjects with and without CVD
events are listed in Table 1. All previously known classical
risk factors for CVD were signiﬁcantly associated with
CVD in this FH patient cohort. In a univariate model, age
presented the strongest association with CVD events ( b-co-
efﬁcient of 0.69) followed by hypertension ( b-coefﬁcient of
0.31) and atherogenic lipoprotein biomarkers such as non-
HDL-cholesterol, ApoB, and LDL-C ( b-coefﬁcients 0.27,
0.24, and 0.23, respectively). HDL-C was the only protec-
tive factor and presented a signiﬁcant inverse relationship
with CVD frequency in FH ( b-coefﬁcient of 20.27).
A multivariate model was developed by stepwise
addition of individual variables to the model ( Table 2). In
this multivariate model, age, gender, hypertension, smok-
ing, and HDL-C remained signiﬁcantly associated with
CVD events, whereas other variables (including athero-
genic lipoprotein biomarkers) did not add signiﬁcantly to
the model. This model explained 43.8% of the variance
observed in CVD frequency in FH subjects.
V ariables signiﬁcantly associated with CVD in the
multivariate model were used to develop the MFHS to
predict CVD events in FH patients ( Table 3 ). The MFHS
presented a Gaussian distribution with a mean of 21.33
and a standard deviation of 8.77 ( Supplemental
Figure II ). The 5th and 95th interval limits of this distribu-
tion were 7 and 37, respectively, and the mean score was 10
points higher in the CVD group than in the non-CVD group
(28.2 6 7.1 and 17.9 6 7.4, respectively). The b-coefﬁcient
of the MFHS for association with CVD was 0.88
(P , .0001). The clinical prediction of the Montreal-FH-
