 SCORE was compared to each of the individual variables
using ROC curves. The AUC of the MFHS was signiﬁ-
cantly greater than that of age alone (0.840 [0.808–0.872]
vs 0.793 [0.757–0.828], P , .0001) or other variable
(Fig 2 ). All AUCs were signiﬁcantly different from 0.5.
Figure 3 A presents the increased prevalence of CVD by
5 points increments of MFHS, whereas Figure 3B compares
the prevalence of CVD in the low score group ( #20 points)
vs high score group [ $21 points]; 10.3% vs 54.1%, respec-
tively ( P , .0001). Subjects in the high score group had a
10.3 times higher odds of presenting CVD events compared
to the low risk group (OR, 10.3; 95% CI, 6.7–15.7,
P , .0001).
Discussion
Previous studies have shown that classical risk factors
such as age, male gender, smoking, hypertension, diabetes,
and lipoprotein parameters are all predictors of CVD risk in
FH populations, 3,20 but no study has looked at the com-
bined effect of these risk factors. We propose a novel clin-
ical score to help predict CVD events in FH patients by
combining classical risk factors. This approach is some-
what similar to the point scoring system used to predict car-
diovascular risk in non-FH patients such as the
Framingham Risk Score, the European SCORE, or the Rey-
nolds Risk Score.
The Montreal-FH-SCORE exhibits a good predictive
accuracy with an AUC of 0.840, which is similar to other
diagnostic scores used in clinic. For comparison, the
Framingham Risk Score had an AUC of 0.776 and 0.834
(for men and women, respectively)
29 for the prediction of
CVD events in the general population. Most of the predic-
tive value of our model originates from age, and this is not
unexpected because the underlying physiopathological
mechanism of CVD in FH is cholesterol-year exposure.
The addition of other variables signiﬁcantly improves the
model’s predictive capacity vs age alone. HDL-C was
strongly and inversely correlated with CVD events. The
relative importance of HDL-C in the Montreal-FH-
SCORE is quite high compared to other risk predictive
scores (Framingham and SCORE). In FH, the extremely
high LDL-C will lead to premature subclinical atheroscle-
rosis, and we believe that reverse cholesterol capacity, rep-
resented by HDL-C, becomes a key factor for limiting the
progression of atherosclerosis and the occurrence of CVD
events.
30 As expected, males were at a signiﬁcantly higher
risk of CVD compared to females with a 1.5-fold increase
in event rates. This is coherent with data from other
studies.
22 The prevalence of hypertension in our cohort
was 3-fold higher in the CVD group in which nearly 1/3
