 of FH subjects had this diagnosis. Smoking was quite prev-
alent in our FH cohort, and this could be due to the fact that
patients were identiﬁed over a long period of time during,
which smoking was more prevalent than today.
It is interesting to note that, in our multivariate analysis,
several important risk factors that have been previously
associated with CVD in FH were no longer independent
predictors. Most importantly, LDL-C, ApoB, or non-HDL-
C did not add signiﬁcantly to our model. This can be
explained by the fact that in both CVD and non-CVD
groups, LDL-C concentrations were already extremely
elevated (7.79 6 1.47 vs 7.17 6 1.47). As previously
stated, the effect of LDL-C was mostly accounted for by
age since lifelong exposure to high LDL-C is the risk factor
primarily explaining the increase in premature CVD in FH.
The addition of Lp(a) to our multivariate model did not
signiﬁcantly add to the prediction of CVD. This could be
due to the fact that the difference in median Lp(a) between
both groups was small (0.61 vs 0.54 mmol/L). Furthermore,
diabetes was not a signiﬁcant predictor of CVD in a
multivariate model. This is most likely due to the low
prevalence of diabetes in our cohort. If the sample size was
larger, diabetes may have become a signiﬁcant risk factor.
However, it has already been reported in a large cross-
sectional study that FH subjects had a lower frequency of
diabetes compared to unaffected relatives (1.75% vs 2.93%,
respectively).
31
Our multivariate model explained 43.8% of the variance
in CVD frequency. According to data from the INTER-
HEART study, psychosocial factors, daily consumption of
fruits and vegetables, regular alcohol consumption, and
regular physical activity represent major and independent
predictors of myocardial infarction. We believe that these
lifestyle factors could account for most of the residual CVD
risk in our FH cohort.
32
There is no doubt that statin therapy should be the
cornerstone of CVD risk reduction in patients with FH.
Indeed, early statin therapy since childhood has been shown
to lower the progression of atherosclerotic plaque in this
disease.
33 Most of the patients in our study had a prior
exposure to statin therapy, and this is most likely due to
the referral bias to our lipid clinic. The prevalence of prior
exposure was similar between the two groups (79% vs
72%) in favor of patients with CVD.
Our study clearly indicates that FH patients with mul-
tiple CVD risk factors present a large increase in frequency
of CVD despite statin therapy. FH patients in primary
prevention with a risk score of 20 or less present a
signiﬁcant lower frequency of CVD events compared to
