 those with a score $21 ( Fig 3 AB). Indeed, patients in the
highest MFHS score group presented a very signiﬁcant
10.3-fold higher odds of presenting CVD events compared
to the low risk group. Those patients with a higher score, as
well as patients in secondary prevention, would most likely
beneﬁt from further LDL-C lowering using novel
therapies in addition to maximally tolerated statin
therapy 6 ezetimibe. Obviously, this proposed scoring sys-
tem does not alter the need to early initiation of intensive
lipid-lowering therapy in all FH patients.
Potential limitations of the study include the fact that
this is a retrospective cohort study, and that the MFHS was
developed to predict prevalent and not incident CVD.
Despite this limitation, the MFHS is very strongly associ-
ated with CVD events. Our results need to be replicated in a
large independent prospective cohort of FH patients before
they are used in a clinical setting. Other limitations of our
study include the deﬁnition for statin use and smoking.
Indeed, the duration and intensity of statin therapy is an
important predictor of CVD events.
33 Unfortunately, this
information was not available in this cohort of patients.
Similarly, all patients with a prior history of smoking
regardless of pack-year exposure were considered smokers.
Unfortunately, more detailed information concerning age of
start, age of cessation, and pack-year exposure to smoking
were not available. Of note, the approach used for the
MFHS is similar to the one used in the widely recognized
Framingham Risk Score
15 Finally, the present study only
included subjects carrying an FH-causing mutation in the
LDLR gene. Whether the MFHS applies to FH caused by
mutations in APOB or PCSK9 genes remains to be
validated.
Conclusions
In conclusion, not all FH patients present the same risk
of CVD. Age, HDL-C, gender, hypertension, and smoking
are important independent predictors of CVD risk. We have
developed a novel tool combining these independent risk
factors to help predict the CVD risk in this population of
patients. As with any risk score, validation in other
populations would be important to conﬁrm its clinical
usefulness. Despite this, the Montreal-FH-SCORE signiﬁ-
cantly stratiﬁes patients in higher and lower CVD groups.
