Statistical analyses
Statistical analyses were performed using SAS 9.4
(SAS Institute, Cary, NC ) and SPSS 20.0 (IBM Corp,
Armonk, NY). A 2-sided P value of #.05 was considered
to indicate statistical signiﬁ cance. Differences in baseline
subjects’ characteristics between the CVD and the
non-CVD groups were evaluated by Student’s t-test
(for continuous variables) or Chi-square test (for categor-
ical variables). V ariables with a skewed distribution (tri-
glycerides, very low-density l ipoprotein cholesterol, and
Lp(a)) were normalized by log transformation prior the
analysis.
The MFHS
13 was calculated for each patients of the vali-
dation cohort according to the original chart of CVD points
(Table 1). We used ROC curves to determine the discrimi-
native power of the MFHS (or C-statistic). A good discrim-
ination was established when AUC value reach $0.75. An
AUC value equal or above 0.80 was considered excel-
lent.
20,21 The method described by Delong et al. (1988)
was used to test the statistical signiﬁcance of the difference
between the ROC curves of each predictors compared with
the ROC curve of the MHFS using MedCalc (MedCalc
Software, Mariakerke, Belgium).
22
In the combined cohort, univariate logistic regression
was performed to rank the CVD predictors using absolute
b-coefﬁcient (standardized coefﬁcient) values. To
determine which variables were included in the
Combined-FH-SCORE, the predictors were entered into a
multivariate regression model in descending order of
absolute univariate b-coefﬁcient values, using a stepwise
method (cut-point of 0.05). The Combined-FH-SCORE
chart was then developed according to the same method-
ology used to develop the MFHS. Brieﬂy, the absolute
multivariate b-coefﬁcient values were used to attribute
points in conjunction to the 5th and 95th percentiles of each
variable (see Paquette et al, 2017).
13
Combined-FH-SCORE and MFHS performances were
assessed by discrimination (ROC analysis). Statistical dif-
ference between both AUC was computed according to the
method described by Delong et al (1988). 22
Results
Description of the validation cohort
In this cohort of 718 FH subjects ( Table 2 ), 37% pre-
sented a history of CVD events. Subjects with CVD events
were signiﬁcantly older (aged 49 vs 38 years, P , .0001),
had a higher prevalence of hypertension (34% vs 13%,
P , .0001), diabetes (9% vs 3%, P 5 .001), and smoking
(74% vs 52%, P , .0001), as well as a higher average body
mass index (26.6 6 4.6 vs 25.4 6 4.7 kg/m
2, P 5 .001)
compared with subjects without CVD events.
Triglycerides were signiﬁcantly higher (1.60 [1.10–2.40]
vs 1.40 [0.90–2.10], P 5 .001) in FH subjects with CVD,
and these subjects had a signiﬁcantly lower HDL-C
(1.07 6 0.33 vs 1.16 6 0.34, P , .0001). No signiﬁcant
differences in LDL-C and Lp(a) were noted.
Supplemental Figure S1 A presents the frequency of
LDLR mutations observed in this cohort. Two mutations
accounted for the vast majority of these FH subjects.
Indeed, 70.6% of FH subjects carried the W66G mutation
of the LDLR and 25.1% carried the del . 15kb mutation
(25.1%).
ROC curve analysis in the validation cohort
The MFHS for CVD was compared with individual
variables using ROC curves. The MFHS presented a
signiﬁcant greater AUC for prediction of prevalent CVD
(0.799) than that of age (0.746, P , .0001), HDL-C (0.578,
P , .0001), gender (0.652, P , .0001), hypertension
